  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Tuesday: A mild, dry Saturday is expected as a \nweak ridge pattern is over the region behind yesterdays cold \nfront. Highs will reach into the 50s. It will be short lived as \nanother Low will slide down the coastline from BC to Oregon \nthrough Sunday. Ensembles have come into better agreement on the\ntiming and track. The Low will slowly track over Oregon through\nMonday. It has potential to bring light showers across the \nInland Northwest. The amounts are not looking to be much as \nprecipitable water amounts struggle to get above half an inch. \nThe daily 25-75 percent probabilities are: 0-0.1 on Sunday, \n0.1-0.3 on Monday, and 0.05-0.2 (highest amounts over Southeast \nWA and Lower ID Panhandle) on Tuesday. Snow levels will be \naround 4500 ft. An inch of snow is possible for the higher \nelevations. Highs will be in the upper 40s to low 60s. Overnight\nlows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Ensembles are indicating another \nsystem pushing into the region from the Gulf of Alaska. Again, \nthere is a lot of uncertainty on the track of the Low. Around 35\npercent of members have the Low over the Pacific Northwest. The\nother 65 percent have a more generally broad trough pattern \nover the region. Either way, there is not a lot of moisture \nassociated with the system. It will push some colder air into \nthe region. Highs will be in the upper 30s to low 50s. Overnight\nlows will be in the 20s to low 30s. /JDC",
